* sg13g2-bandgap -- closed-loop AC output-impedance-at-vref PEX (post-layout)
* PVT testbench (issue #191, independent confirmation of the
* closed-loop-psrr-pex ~30 MHz PSRR dip). Generated from
* sim/closed-loop-zout-pex/testbench/tb_closed_loop_zout_pex.spice.tmpl by
* sim/closed-loop-zout-pex/run_pvt_sweep.sh -- do not edit a generated
* netlist-snapshots/*.spice file directly, edit this template and re-run the
* sweep. Full rationale is in sim/closed-loop-zout-pex/README.md; this header
* is a pointer, not a restatement.
*
* Generated for: corner=fs temp=125C vdd=3.30V
* DUT git sha (design/netlist/bandgap_core.spice, for XQ1/XQ2/XQ3, the
* only devices this bench still splices from a schematic):
*   core=d83f7c4 amp=397fe2d startup=66ac99d seeds=20260830-132425-d83f7c4.csv@94207b4
* Layout git sha (layout/bandgap_top/bandgap_top.pex.spice, extraction
* input for every MOS/resistor device and every wire parasitic):
*   8abe2aa
* PDK: ihp-sg13g2 /home/ubuntu/share/pdk (see sim/pdk.json for the pinned release)
* OSDI models: /home/ubuntu/share/pdk/ihp-sg13g2/libs.tech/ngspice/osdi (built by sim/tools/build-osdi.sh)
*
* WHY THIS BENCH EXISTS -- issue #191 asks for an independent confirmation,
* distinct from sim/closed-loop-psrr-pex's own supply-injection measurement,
* of whether that experiment's 27.1-31.6 MHz worst-case-PSRR dip
* (records/20260905-233826-f6f3efc.csv) is the closed loop's OWN resonance
* interacting with the extracted wire parasitics, or an artifact of
* injecting the stimulus on vdd specifically (upstream of the extracted
* supply-wire resistance/capacitance). This bench answers that by measuring
* a DIFFERENT closed-loop transfer function that shares the same
* "1/(1+T(s))" return-difference denominator PSRR does, but is stimulated
* at a completely different port: instead of a 1V/0deg AC perturbation on
* vdd with the output read at vref (PSRR), this bench injects a 1A/0deg AC
* TEST CURRENT directly at vref (vdd is now a plain DC source, unperturbed)
* and reads the resulting v(vref) -- i.e. the closed-loop small-signal
* output impedance Zout(f) = v(vref)/i(Itest) looking into vref. Both PSRR
* and Zout degrade (peak/notch) together exactly where the loop's own
* return difference 1+T(jw) gets small -- that is a property of the loop
* itself, not of which port happens to be stimulated. If Zout ALSO peaks
* near 27-31 MHz on this same extracted netlist, that is evidence the
* mechanism is the loop's own resonance (present regardless of injection
* port); if Zout stays flat/monotonic through that band while PSRR still
* dips there, that would instead point at something specific to the vdd
* injection path (e.g. the extracted vdd wire network's own resonance).
* See README.md "Why an output-impedance probe" for the full argument.
*
* THIS IS THE DEVICE/PARASITIC HALF verbatim of
* sim/closed-loop-psrr-pex/testbench/tb_closed_loop_psrr_pex.spice.tmpl
* (itself the device/parasitic half of
* sim/closed-loop-vref-pvt-pex/testbench/tb_closed_loop_vref_pex.spice.tmpl,
* same extraction, same instance names, same wire-parasitic block) -- every
* device card and every wire-parasitic line below is byte-for-byte identical
* to that file; only the injection/measurement FIXTURE and the .control
* block differ. This bench does NOT break the loop anywhere (unlike
* sim/loop-gain-phase-margin's Middlebrook probe) -- fb remains one single
* node exactly as bandgap_top.sch wires it and exactly as the extraction's
* own single "fb" hub node models it. Everything
* sim/closed-loop-vref-pvt-pex/README.md documents about what the extraction
* does and does not model (bipolar devices spliced in, D/S terminal roles
* possibly swapped, body/well ties real, the three merged-net labels) and
* everything sim/closed-loop-psrr-pex/README.md documents about the vsubs
* tie choice applies here unchanged and is not restated.
*
* DELTA vs. tb_closed_loop_psrr_pex.spice.tmpl -- exactly two:
*   1. Vvdd carries ONLY the fixed DC operating point here (no AC term) --
*      vdd is not the stimulus in this bench.
*   2. Itest is a new 1A/0deg AC current source injected directly at vref
*      (DC = 0, so it does not perturb the .nodeset-seeded DC operating
*      point at all); v(vref) is read directly as the complex impedance
*      Zout(f), since a 1A magnitude stimulus makes v(vref) numerically
*      equal to Zout in ohms.
.nodeset v(fb)=2.52724e+00 v(sns1)=5.90124e-01 v(sns2)=5.89840e-01 v(vref)=1.04761e+00

.lib "/home/ubuntu/share/pdk/ihp-sg13g2/libs.tech/ngspice/models/cornerHBT.lib" hbt_typ
.lib "/home/ubuntu/share/pdk/ihp-sg13g2/libs.tech/ngspice/models/cornerMOShv.lib" mos_fs
.lib "/home/ubuntu/share/pdk/ihp-sg13g2/libs.tech/ngspice/models/cornerRES.lib" res_typ

.options temp=125 tnom=27

* FIXTURE -- vdd is a FIXED DC source here (DELTA 1 above): this bench's
* stimulus is Itest at vref, not vdd. Vvss is a separate, unperturbed 0V
* source, same as every other closed-loop bench in this tree.
Vvdd vdd 0 dc 3.30
Vvss vss 0 dc 0

* FIXTURE -- substrate/far-plate return for every extracted ground
* capacitance below, shorted to vss -- same choice and same rationale as
* sim/closed-loop-psrr-pex (see that experiment's README "The vsubs tie
* matters here"): a frequency-domain bench cannot use the transient/DC PEX
* benches' 1 TOhm DC-only tie, because at AC that would leave vsubs
* effectively floating and turn every net's ground cap into a net-to-net
* coupling cap instead of a cap to ground. A 0V source is a DC and AC short
* and injects nothing of its own.
Vvsubs vsubs vss dc 0

* STIMULUS -- Itest injects a 1A/0deg AC test current directly into the
* vref node (DC = 0, so the .nodeset-seeded DC operating point above is
* undisturbed). "0 vref" ordering: SPICE convention is that current flows
* from N+ to N- THROUGH the source, so externally it is delivered TO N-
* (vref) FROM N+ (here node 0) -- i.e. this pushes 1A of test current INTO
* vref from outside the loop, exactly the classic single-current-injection
* output-impedance probe. Because the loop is fully closed here (unlike
* sim/loop-gain-phase-margin's broken-loop Vtest), v(vref)/i(Itest) is the
* CLOSED-LOOP output impedance Zout(f), not an open-loop quantity.
Itest 0 vref dc 0 ac 1 0

